From a099c15d42ccc5aad5ef16446027884cbfc5e21c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ryoma-89 <ryoma-89@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 03:38:11 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 19/20] velox: ASG prefetch trace, adaptive mode and thread
 placement (patch 0019)

Diagnostics and experiments for the receive prefetch (0018), all opt-in:
- VELOX_ASG_PREFETCH_TRACE=<prefix>: per-context timeline of RenderThread
  acquires, views, direct receives and prefetch pumps, written at teardown
  (scripts/analyze_prefetch_trace.py in Velox reads it).
- VELOX_ASG_PREFETCH_AHEAD=3 (adaptive): a large view keeps the stream hot
  for VELOX_ASG_PREFETCH_HOLD_US; while hot the prefetch thread keeps
  reading across view boundaries, may poll more often
  (VELOX_ASG_PREFETCH_HOT_POLL), and RenderThread may wait for an
  in-flight packet instead of taking the rings over
  (VELOX_ASG_PREFETCH_WAIT_US). Measured: no gain over AHEAD=2 (the
  remaining direct receives wait for the guest to produce the data) and
  sync -5%, so it stays an experiment. AHEAD=2 remains the default.
- Thread names (gfxstream-render, velox-asg-prefetch) and optional
  affinity from VELOX_RENDER_AFFINITY / VELOX_PREFETCH_AFFINITY (hex masks
  the launcher derives from EfficiencyClass, ANDed with the process mask).
- VELOX_ASG_PROFILE also samples how often each thread runs on a core of
  the highest EfficiencyClass.
The diagnostic clock reads in receiveDirect only happen when profiling or
tracing. Ring order, checks and replies are unchanged.

